## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift in embeddings over time and trigger re-embedding schedules?
- What tenancy/ACL considerations would you enforce for cross-repo queries?
- How would you structure A/B tests for recall vs latency trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to engineer a practical, scalable evaluation system for real-world multilingual policy assistants operating under complex brand-specific constraints with live documentation dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual policy alignment across distinct brand ecosystems
- Versioned, source-grounded citation verification and tracking
- Cross-brand policy conflict detection and temporal drift analysis
- Lightweight evaluation datasets with granular per-claim scoring mechanisms

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example citation validation function
function validateCitationCurrentness(claim, policyIndex) {
  const citation = claim?.citation;
  const policy = policyIndex.find(p => p.id === citation?.policyId);
  return policy && policy.version === citation?.version && policy.isActive;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests production-grade RAG design across multilingual, heterogeneous corpora with multi-tenant isolation and performance constraints at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Hierarchical chunking (document → sections → paragraphs) with size optimization
- Multilingual embeddings and incremental refresh pipelines
- Vector DB partitioning with ACLs per tenant
- Two-stage retrieval with semantic search and cross-encoder reranking
- Freshness management, latency budgets (200-500ms), and comprehensive failover strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch for chunking and indexing
function chunkDocument(text, maxBytes = 4000) {
  const chunks = [];
  let current = '';

  // Split by paragraphs and respect semantic boundaries
  const paragraphs = text.split(/\n\s*\n/);

  for (const paragraph of paragraphs) {
    if (current.length + paragraph.length > maxBytes) {
      if (current) chunks.push(current.trim());
      current = paragraph;
    } else {
      current += (current ? '\n\n' : '') + paragraph;
    }
  }

  if (current) chunks.push(current.trim());
  return chunks;
}

// Tenant-aware retrieval
async function retrieveChunks(query, tenantId, limit = 5) {
  const embeddedQuery = await embedQuery(query);
  const results = await vectorStore.search({
    vector: embeddedQuery,
    filter: { tenantId },
    limit: limit * 2 // Get more for reranking
  });

  // Rerank with cross-encoder
  return await rerank(query, results).slice(0, limit);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates a concrete, beginner-friendly plan to evaluate how tokenization choices influence attention in a multilingual setting, including preserving domain terms and generating actionable metrics.

## Key Concepts

- Tiny Transformer architectures and tokenization workflows
- Attention maps and per-token signaling across languages
- Tokenization strategies: BPE vs unigram and their impact on domain terms
- Evaluation metrics and masking rules for brand terms

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-runner to compute per-prompt attention-signal
function attentionSignal(attentions, sentimentIndices) {
  // attentions: [layers][heads][from][to], sentimentIndices: array
  let total = 0, count = 0;
  for (const layer of attentions) {
    for (const head of layer) {
      for (const idx of sentimentIndices) {
        for (const j of head) {
          total += j[idx];
          count++;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return count ? total / count : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- What edge cases could bias the attention-signal, and how would you mitigate them?
- How would you extend this to assess zero-shot cross-language generalization?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to architect a scalable cross-lingual RAG pipeline handling chunking, multilingual embeddings, and vector stores with versioning and TTL. It also probes cross-language retrieval strategies and evaluation plans to reduce hallucinations.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual embeddings (LaBSE, mBERT) and language tagging
- Document-type aware chunking strategies
- Vector DB indexing with versioning, TTL/decay, and provenance
- Cross-language retrieval + fallback translation
- Evaluation: latency, recall across languages, hallucination rate

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: bilingual embedder + cross-language retrieve
const embedder = new LaBSEEmbedder({ model: 'labse' })
const index = new VectorDB({ name: 'docs', ... })
async function ingest(doc){
  const chunks = chunkDoc(doc, typeRules[doc.type])
  for (const c of chunks){
    index.upsert({ id: c.id, vec: embedder.embed(c.text), meta: { lang: c.lang, version: doc.version, ts: doc.ts }})
  }
}
async function query(q, lang){
  const v = embedder.embed(q)
  const hits = index.query(v, { k: 5, lang })
  // cross-language re-rank can involve translating top hits or language-agnostic scoring
  return hits
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle new languages or drift in embeddings?
- How would you measure cross-language retrieval effectiveness in production?
